      <h2>What Is a Cat Logic Puzzle?</h2>
      <p>A <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> is a grid-based deduction game inspired by the classic Japanese puzzle Akari (Light Up), created by <a href="https://www.nikoli.co.jp/en/">Nikoli</a>. In GridPaw's cat-themed version, you place adorable glowing cats on a grid instead of light bulbs. Each cat illuminates its entire row and column until blocked by a wall. Your goal: light up every white cell while following numbered clues on black walls.</p>
      <p>Unlike Sudoku, which relies on number placement, a <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> uses <strong>spatial reasoning</strong>. You think in terms of lines of sight, adjacency constraints, and elimination — all without any arithmetic. The cat theme makes it approachable and cozy, perfect for a relaxing <strong>brain training</strong> session. Whether you are a seasoned puzzle veteran or a complete beginner, the <strong>cat puzzle game</strong> format on GridPaw offers a warm, inviting entry point into the world of logic puzzles.</p>
      <p>The concept is simple: numbered black cells tell you how many of their four adjacent cells must contain cats. No two cats can see each other along the same row or column. Every white cell must be illuminated. That is the entire ruleset — but the deductions it enables can be surprisingly deep and satisfying.</p>

      <h2>How to Play Cat Logic Puzzles</h2>
      <p>Playing a <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> on GridPaw is intuitive, even if you have never tried an Akari-style puzzle before:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Click a white cell</strong> to place a glowing cat. Click the same cell again to remove it.</li>
        <li>Each cat <strong>illuminates its entire row and column</strong> — light travels outward until it hits a black wall or the grid edge. Illuminated cells glow with a warm golden color.</li>
        <li><strong>Numbered black cells</strong> are your clues. A "2" means exactly 2 of its 4 adjacent cells must contain cats. A "0" means none of its neighbors can have a cat.</li>
        <li><strong>No two cats can see each other</strong> — if two cats are in the same row or column with no wall between them, that placement is illegal and the grid will warn you.</li>
        <li><strong>All white cells must be illuminated</strong> to win the puzzle.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>On mobile, <strong>long-press</strong> a cell to mark it with a paw print — useful for noting cells you know cannot have cats. On desktop, right-click or double-click to toggle the paw mark. This annotation system is essential for harder puzzles where tracking impossibilities becomes critical.</p>

      <h2>Why Cats Make Puzzles Better</h2>
      <p>You might wonder: why add cats to a logic puzzle? The answer goes beyond aesthetics. The cat theme in this <strong>cat puzzle game</strong> serves several practical purposes that genuinely improve the solving experience:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lower intimidation barrier</strong> — A grid full of cute cats looks like play, not homework. Players who might shy away from a "logic puzzle" happily engage with a "cat puzzle." This makes the <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> format ideal for introducing friends and family to deduction games.</li>
        <li><strong>Emotional warmth</strong> — The warm golden glow of illuminated cells and the round, friendly cat icons create a cozy atmosphere. This transforms solving from a stressful test into a meditative, calming activity. Many players describe the experience as "digital hygge."</li>
        <li><strong>Better visual feedback</strong> — Cat icons are larger and more distinct than plain light bulb symbols, making it easier to scan the grid at a glance. The visual hierarchy (cats vs illuminated vs dark cells) is clearer with the cat theme than with traditional Akari graphics.</li>
        <li><strong>Memorable identity</strong> — There are hundreds of generic Akari clones online. The cat theme gives GridPaw a distinct personality that players remember and return to. A <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> is not just another grid — it is a cozy world you step into.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The cat theme also makes this <strong>logic puzzle</strong> more shareable. "I just solved a cat logic puzzle" is a more engaging social media post than "I completed a constraint satisfaction problem." The whimsical framing invites curiosity and lowers the social barrier to sharing puzzle achievements.</p>

      <h2>Difficulty Levels</h2>
      <p>GridPaw offers three difficulty tiers for the <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong>, each designed for a different skill level:</p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:.8rem 0;font-size:.95rem;">
        <thead><tr style="border-bottom:2px solid #E8A888;text-align:left;"><th style="padding:.4rem;">Level</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Grid</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Techniques Needed</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Best For</th></tr></thead>
