        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>If you are new to the <strong>cat puzzle game</strong> format, start with Easy. The 7×7 grid has more numbered clues relative to its size, which means more forced placements and fewer dead ends. You will build intuition quickly. Once you can solve Easy puzzles without hints, move to Medium. Hard puzzles are for players who have mastered all the techniques in the strategy guide below.</p>

      <h2>Strategy Guide for Cat Logic Puzzles</h2>
      <h3>Beginner: Start with Extremes</h3>
      <p>The fastest way to begin any <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> is to scan for extreme clues:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"4" cells</strong> — all four neighbors must be cats. Place them immediately. This is the single most powerful starting move in any <strong>logic puzzle</strong> of this type.</li>
        <li><strong>"0" cells</strong> — none of its neighbors can have cats. Mark all four adjacent cells with paw prints right away to eliminate them from consideration.</li>
        <li><strong>Work from corners and edges</strong> — a corner cell can only be lit from two directions, and an edge cell from three. These constrained positions often lead to forced placements early in the solve.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Intermediate: Constraint Propagation</h3>
      <p>Once you have placed all obvious cats from extreme clues, move to constraint propagation:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Count remaining neighbors</strong> — if a "3" cell has two neighbors already blocked (by walls, paw marks, or conflicting cats), the remaining two must both be cats.</li>
        <li><strong>Line-of-sight elimination</strong> — once you place a cat, every cell in its row and column cannot hold another cat. This often forces placements in distant parts of the grid.</li>
        <li><strong>Shadow mapping</strong> — track which cells are illuminated. A dark cell that can only be lit from one remaining direction is a forced placement. This technique is the bread and butter of Medium puzzles.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Advanced: Elimination Reasoning</h3>
      <p>On Hard <strong>cat logic puzzles</strong>, simple propagation may not be enough. Use these advanced techniques:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hypothetical deduction</strong> — pick an undecided cell and mentally assume "a cat goes here." Propagate all consequences. If you reach a contradiction (a numbered cell gets too many cats, or a white cell becomes impossible to light), then your assumption was wrong and that cell cannot hold a cat.</li>
        <li><strong>Chain reasoning</strong> — placing one cat forces or eliminates others in a cascade. Follow the consequences as far as they go before making your next move.</li>
        <li><strong>Shadow map narrowing</strong> — for each undecided cell, track which directions could still illuminate it. As the grid fills in, the shadow map narrows until only one option remains.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Expert: Pattern Recognition</h3>
      <p>After solving dozens of <strong>cat logic puzzles</strong>, you will start recognizing common patterns. A "2" cell in a corner with one wall neighbor almost always forces a specific configuration. A row of three consecutive white cells between two walls has limited valid arrangements. These pattern libraries build naturally through practice and dramatically speed up your solving.</p>

      <h2>Why Cat Logic Puzzles Are Good for Brain Training</h2>
      <p>A <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> exercises multiple cognitive skills simultaneously, making it one of the most efficient forms of <strong>brain training</strong> available in a casual game format:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spatial reasoning</strong> — you must visualize lines of sight across the entire grid, tracking which cells are illuminated and which remain dark.</li>
        <li><strong>Logical deduction</strong> — every placement must be justified by a chain of reasoning. Guessing leads to contradictions; only logic leads to solutions.</li>
        <li><strong>Pattern recognition</strong> — experienced solvers recognize common configurations instantly, building a mental library of puzzle patterns.</li>
        <li><strong>Working memory</strong> — you must hold multiple constraints in mind simultaneously: which numbered cells are not yet satisfied, which cells are still dark, which positions are impossible.</li>
        <li><strong>Patience and focus</strong> — unlike timed games, the <strong>cat puzzle game</strong> rewards careful, unhurried thinking. There is no clock, no penalty for taking your time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Research suggests that regularly engaging with <strong>logic puzzles</strong> like Akari-style games can help maintain cognitive flexibility and processing speed. The cozy cat theme adds an emotional comfort layer that makes the <strong>brain training</strong> feel like self-care rather than work. Many players describe their daily <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> session as a form of meditation — focused, calming, and deeply satisfying when the final cat lights up the last dark cell.</p>

      <h2>Cat Logic Puzzle vs Other Puzzles</h2>
      <p>How does the <strong>cat logic puzzle</strong> compare to other popular puzzle types?</p>
      <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:.8rem 0;font-size:.95rem;">
        <thead><tr style="border-bottom:2px solid #E8A888;text-align:left;"><th style="padding:.4rem;">Feature</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Cat Logic Puzzle</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Sudoku</th><th style="padding:.4rem;">Nonograms</th></tr></thead>
